\b\f39\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15336604\charrsid12259670  }{\f39\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15336604\charrsid12259670 What is the }{\b\f39\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15336604\charrsid12259670 this }{\f39\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15336604\charrsid12259670 pointer? Give an example of its use}{
\f39\cf0\ulc0\insrsid615578 
\par }{\f0\cf18\insrsid615578\charrsid615578 In a C++ program, if you create object A of class X, you can then obtain the address of A by using the "this" pointer. The address is ava
ilable as a local variable in the non-static member functions of X, and its type is const X*. The "this" pointer works because C++ creates instances of its data members, and it keeps one copy of each member function}{
\f0\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15336604\charrsid615578 .
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f39\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15336604\charrsid12259670 
