\par }{\cs32\f0\insrsid15534637\charrsid15534637 malloc}{\f0\insrsid15534637\charrsid15534637  returns a void pointer to the allocated space or }{\cs32\f0\insrsid15534637\charrsid15534637 NULL}{\f0\insrsid15534637\charrsid15534637 
 if there is insufficient memory available. To return a pointer to a type other than }{\cs32\f0\insrsid15534637\charrsid15534637 void}{\f0\insrsid15534637\charrsid15534637 
, use a type cast on the return value. The storage space pointed to by the return value is guaranteed to be suitably aligned for storage of any type of object. If size is 0, }{\cs32\f0\insrsid15534637\charrsid15534637 malloc}{
\f0\insrsid15534637\charrsid15534637  allocates a zero-length item in the heap and returns a valid pointer to that item.}{\f0\insrsid15534637 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid7292377 {\f0\insrsid7292377\charrsid7292377 
By default, malloc does not call the new handler routine on failure to allocate memory. You can override this default behavior so that, when malloc fails to allocate memory, malloc calls the new handler routine in the same way that the new oper
ator does when it fails for the same reason}{\f0\insrsid7292377 .}{\f0\insrsid7292377\charrsid7292377 
